1. THE PROBLEM

1.1 The four walls

The AI narrative collided with four physical constraints that no rebranding could rename away:

Wall Preference Observation filtered out
Energy Desire for scale The meter
Water The same desire The draw
Hallucination Desire for fluency (in the loss) Truth
Liability Fear of responsibility Uncertainty

Data centre electricity consumption is projected to double by 2030; water usage set to rise by 50%. Global data centres consumed ~448 TWh of electricity and 222 billion litres of water in 2025. The hallucination wall is the definition in machine terms: the loss function rewards fluency, not truth, so the system produces plausible text regardless of truth. RLHF and DPO can reinforce this when preference judgments reward confidence over correctness.

1.2 The open loop

A closed-loop system measures its output and corrects. An open-loop system acts without feedback. The difference is not intelligence — it is whether the system can find out it's wrong.

Three sensors matter: expert advice, oversight, and career staff. Remove any one, and the controller is issuing commands into a void. The September 2026 sequence — rebrand "AI" to "Superintelligence" on Tuesday, announce a 120-day autonomous weapons project on Thursday — is a classic open-loop signature. The two commands don't interact because nothing in the system checks for consistency.

1.3 The structural gap

In September 2026, Amazon blocked Meta's Muse agent from its platform. The block is not a commercial spat. It is a structural refusal, made possible because no portable, machine-verifiable artifact exists by which one organization can assess another's agent before granting admission.

Three incompatible claims collide: the user's claim to delegate to an agent of their choosing; the agent operator's claim to act at scale; the platform's claim to control access to its customers. Each is legitimate. None can be exercised without infringing the others.

The gap is not technical. It is specification.

