2. ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW

ST-T1024 is a deterministic inference architecture for embedded and edge hardware. Its defining commitments:

2.1 Determinism by construction. Temperature fixed at 0.0. Same input, same state, same output, bit-identical. Simulation equals benchmarking.

2.2 FIS triage. Inputs routed to exactly one domain tile by a fuzzy inference system with ROM-burned membership functions and rule base.

2.3 FSM scheduling. Execution under a finite state machine with a declared Assured Execution Time (AET).

2.4 Ternary logic. Values in {−1, 0, +1}. XNOR + popcount arithmetic. No floating-point multipliers in the hot path.

2.5 Tile independence. Tiles share no knowledge by reference. Shared facts are duplicated in each tile, independently signed and attested.

2.6 Refusal as first-class output. 0x-AMBIG is not an error condition. It is the safety state.

2.7 Rate-graded knowledge. ROM holds the invariant substrate; SRAM holds the certified revisable layer. The boundary is rate of change.

2.8 Open knowledge base. Additions and rectifications arrive continuously, signed, versioned, attributable, revocable, deprecatable.

The core claim: the system's behavior is deterministic, finite, and enumerable — regardless of the internal character of the underlying model.

---

3. DETERMINISM BY CONSTRUCTION

3.1 The six-step pipeline

```
input
→ harvest (normalize; deterministic; no allocation)
→ FIS triage (route to exactly one tile)
→ FSM schedule (bounded cycle counter)
→ tile execute (ternary matcher; fixed function)
→ render (human-readable sentence; deterministic)
→ power down (0 V idle)
```

Every stage is a function. Same input, same output, always. There is no sampler anywhere.

3.2 FIS triage is deterministic

A Fuzzy Inference System has four stages, each a fixed computation:

1. Fuzzification — membership functions are ROM-burned. Given x, μ_A(x) is fixed.
2. Rule evaluation — rule base is ROM-burned. Fixed T-norm. Bounded rule count.
3. Aggregation — fixed S-norm.
4. Defuzzification — fixed method (Sugeno with singleton consequents and weighted average).

No sampling. No runtime learning. No randomness.

The condition that matters: the arithmetic must be exact. Integer or ternary, not floating-point. Float determinism is platform-dependent: ARM FPU ≠ RISC-V F/D ≠ soft-float. The same query would route to different tiles on different hardware. ST-T1024 uses integer or ternary arithmetic, so the FIS is bit-identical across platforms.

3.3 Ternary logic

Dual-plane encoding: each trit is two bits, (pos, neg).

pos neg value
0 0 0
1 0 +1
0 1 −1
1 1 invalid — fail-closed

Dot product via popcount:

```c
acc += popcount(ap & bp);   // (+1)*(+1) = +1
acc += popcount(an & bn);   // (-1)*(-1) = +1
acc -= popcount(ap & bn);   // (+1)*(-1) = -1
acc -= popcount(an & bp);   // (-1)*(+1) = -1
```

No floating-point. No multipliers in the hot loop. On a Cortex-M7 at 200 MHz, a 256-trit dot product completes in ~180 cycles.

