4. TILE INDEPENDENCE

4.1 The expert systems lesson

Early expert systems — MYCIN, DENDRAL, XCON — separated the inference engine from the knowledge base. Correct design. But they collapsed under the knowledge acquisition bottleneck: rules proliferated, interactions multiplied, verification couldn't keep pace. The knowledge base became a liability.

The failure mode was combinatorial interaction in a shared rule space. Rule A and rule B produced behavior neither author predicted. Add rule C, and the interaction surface triples.

4.2 How ST-T1024 prevents it

Tiles are independent by construction.

· Only one tile is powered on at a time.
· Tiles share no knowledge by reference.
· Facts common to multiple tiles are duplicated in each, independently signed and attested.
· Adding tile N+1 touches nothing in tiles 1…N.

This is the share-nothing architecture applied to knowledge. Same pattern as microservices with duplicated data instead of a shared database.

The consequence: the expert systems failure mode is architecturally impossible. Tile count is not a constraint. The system scales to arbitrary tile count.

4.3 Consistency of duplicated facts

Duplication introduces consistency as the new problem. If "conservation of energy" is in tiles 1, 7, and 43, and the country authority revises it, all three copies must update.

The mechanism:

· Every shared fact carries a canonical hash.
· The country authority issues the canonical version.
· Every tile containing that fact carries the same hash.
· The audit ledger verifies cross-tile consistency at every Friday session.
· Divergence → refusal (0x-AMBIG-CONSISTENCY).

This is independence with consistency, enforced by signing and audit.

