5. AET — ASSURED EXECUTION TIME

5.1 The derivation

AET is derivable from architectural constants, not asserted.

Tile independence bounds execution to a single tile (or a bounded call-graph depth if A6 control-flow invocation is used). There is no cross-tile interaction of facts.

FIS triage is deterministic — a fixed computation over ROM-burned rules. T_triage is a declared constant.

Therefore:

AET_total = T_triage + max over tiles of T_tile

If A6 control-flow invocation is used with ROM-burned call graph depth D:

AET_total ≤ D × T_tile_max + T_triage

Either way, both terms are architectural constants. Both are verifiable pre-deployment.

5.2 Why this matters for certification

A stochastic system cannot have AET, because its worst case is "the model generated tokens until it stopped." There is no bound by construction.

Tile independence is what gives you a bound. The FSM is the enforcement mechanism; independence is what makes the enforcement meaningful.

Each tile is certified once. Composition is multiplication by a declared constant. This is what makes ASIL-D, SIL, and DO-178C certification tractable.

5.3 The architectural constant

WCET_CYCLES = 800 is the architectural budget.

Clock WCET AET (measured 680 cycles)
1 GHz (silicon) 800 ns 680 ns
480 MHz (Cortex-M7) 1.67 µs 1.42 µs
200 MHz (typical MCU) 4.0 µs 3.4 µs
100 MHz (minimum) 8.0 µs 6.8 µs

The architecture is unchanged. Only the wall-clock number moves. Every timing claim is stated in cycles first.

5.4 Why caches and RTOS break WCET

D-cache hit/miss timing depends on access history, which depends on input, which varies. WCET analysis with caches is an open research problem. Disable D-cache or use TCM exclusively. This is not optional.

A tick-based RTOS cannot schedule at sub-microsecond granularity. A 1 ms tick is 1,250× the 800-cycle budget at 1 GHz. For certified use, bare metal only.

