7. THE UPDATE PROTOCOL

Three clocks. One discipline.

7.1 Friday session — weekly

New facts enter the SRAM layer. Each addition or rectification is:

· Proposed by an Author
· Verified by a Verifier
· Approved by an Approver

Three distinct roles. Joint and several liability. Veto requires written justification that enters the audit log. If three qualified signers are not available, the session does not occur — 0x-AMBIG-STAFFING.

7.2 Triennial clustering — every three years

Aggregated refusal logs from certified deployments (order of trillions of queries) are deterministically clustered to find:

· New tiles — domains the current structure doesn't cover
· Split tiles — domains that have grown too broad
· Merged tiles — domains that have converged

Deterministic constraints:

· Fixed initial centroids (not random)
· Exact integer or ternary arithmetic
· Bounded iteration
· No random sampling
· Hash-committed corpus before clustering

The proposed structure is signed by the country authority and supersedes the previous ROM burn. The old burn is deprecated, not erased.

7.3 Emergency tiles — on discontinuity

When a query pattern emerges that no existing tile covers, an emergency tile may be created by a named authority.

Properties:

· Declared scope, signed and bounded
· Fast revision clock (weekly or on-demand)
· Refusal as default under source disagreement
· Mandatory sunset date
· Graduation to permanent requires explicit attestation; default is expiry
· Does not override existing tiles
· Same FSM, ROM/SRAM split, refusal semantics, audit ledger

The COVID case:

· January 2020: Emergency tile created. No certified sources. Tile refuses everything.
· March 2020: Sources contradict each other. Layer 2 of the EAR refuses. Contradiction logged.
· June 2020: Certified knowledge emerges. Tile answers within scope, refuses outside it.
· 2023: PHEIC declared over. Tile expires (default) or graduates (explicit attestation).

